 ### SVBRDF with Sparse Latent Space
-Represents Spatially Varying BRDF (SVBRDF) using an encoder-decoder network with a sparse latent space to impose a prior that real-world objects consist of limited types of materials, alleviating noisy roughness caused by lack of supervision [S2]. It transforms input surface point $\mathbf{x}$ to latent code $\mathbf{z}$, then decodes $\mathbf{z}$ to diffuse albedo and roughness [S2]. It uses a sparsity constraint loss $\mathcal{L}_{KL} = \sum_{j=1}^n \text{KL}(\rho \parallel \hat{\rho}_j)$ where $\rho=0.05$ and $\hat{\rho}_j$ is the average of the $j$-th channel of $\mathbf{z}$ over the batch [S2]. The KL divergence is defined as $\text{KL}(\rho \parallel \hat{\rho}_j) = \rho \log(\rho/\hat{\rho}_j) + (1-\rho) \log((1-\rho)/(1-\hat{\rho}_j))$ [S2]. It also applies a smooth loss $\mathcal{L}_s = \| D(\mathbf{z}) - D(\mathbf{z} + \boldsymbol{\xi}) \|_1$ where $\boldsymbol{\xi}$ is noise drawn from $\mathcal{N}(0, 0.01^2)$ [S2]. The decoder is a 2-layer network with 32-dimensional input and 128 hidden units [S2]. It uses Simplified Disney BRDF with fixed $F_0=0.02$ [S2].
+This variant represents Spatially Varying BRDF (SVBRDF) using an encoder-decoder network with a sparse latent space to impose a prior that real-world objects consist of limited types of materials [S2]. It alleviates noisy roughness caused by lack of supervision by transforming the input surface point $x$ to a latent code $z$ using an encoder, then decoding $z$ to diffuse albedo and roughness [S2]. The sparsity constraint is enforced via Kullback-Leibler divergence: $\ell_{KL} = \sum_{j=1}^n KL(\rho || \hat{\rho}_j)$, where $\rho = 0.05$ and $\hat{\rho}_j$ is the average of the $j$-th channel of $z$ over the batch [S2]. A smooth loss $\ell_s = || D(z) - D(z + \xi) ||_1$ is applied, where $\xi$ is drawn from a normal distribution with zero mean and 0.01 variance [S2]. The decoder is a 2-layer network with 32-dimensional input and 128 hidden units, using the Simplified Disney BRDF model with fixed $F_0 = 0.02$ [S2].
 
